// wdoc section: Text & Inline Patterns.
page wdoc_text { sites = [:reference]
  h1 "Text & Inline Patterns"

  p "Most prose is authored as `p` blocks; the body text runs through an inline pattern engine that recognises markdown-style formatting, links, icons, and math. For a paragraph that needs more than one styled segment, drop down to a `text` block holding one or more `span`s."

  h2 "Paragraphs with p"

  p "`p \"...\" {}` is the canonical paragraph shorthand. The label text is the paragraph's content, and inline patterns light up automatically."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
p "Quick prose with **bold** and `inline code`."
p "A whole paragraph in one line."
WCL
  }

  p "A `p` renders as `<p>...inline-formatted content...</p>`. Apply a class to the paragraph itself with `class = [\"…\"]`."

  h2 "Inline patterns"

  p "The following patterns are recognised in any `p` body, in spans inside a `text` block, and in `utf8` table cells:"
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
**bold**             // → bold class
_italic_             // → italic class
`code`               // → monospace inline code
[text](page)         // → in-site link to a page
[text](site:page)    // → cross-site link
[text](https://...)  // → external link
:lucide.check:       // → inline icon (pack.name)
$x^2$                // → inline LaTeX (text style)
$$\int x \, dx$$     // → display-style LaTeX
WCL
  }

  callout "Where inline patterns run" {
    class = ["info"]
    body  = "p bodies, span bodies, and utf8 table cells all run through the inline engine. Callout bodies and heading text do NOT — they render as literal prose. Use class-driven spans inside a text block for emphasis there."
  }

  h2 "Multi-span paragraphs"

  p "Reach for the long form when one paragraph needs several differently-classed segments — for example, an accent word in the middle of plain prose."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
class accent {
  color = "var(--wdoc-accent)"
  bold  = true
}

text {
  span "Normal "
  span "highlighted" { class = ["accent"] }
  span " then normal again."
}
WCL
  }

  p "Each `span` carries its own optional `class` list. Use the multi-span form only when you actually need that per-segment styling; otherwise `p` is shorter and produces less DOM."

  p "Next: [Headings](wdoc_headings)."
}
